# ==============================================================================
# CodeQuest environment
#
# Everything works with NO configuration at all: `npm install && npm run dev`
# gives you accounts, progress, the leaderboard, JavaScript execution in a Node
# sandbox and Python through WebAssembly. Copy this file to `.env` only if you
# want one of the optional extras below. The API, `npm start` and Vite all read
# .env; VITE_API_PROXY follows API_PORT automatically if you leave it unset.
# ==============================================================================

# --- API server ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Port for the local API (server/). Defaults to 4000.
# Note: this is API_PORT, not PORT - dev harnesses set PORT for the web server.
# API_PORT=4000

# Signing key for session tokens (both learner and admin sessions - see
# server/auth.js). One is generated into server/data/.jwt-secret on first run,
# so you only need this in production.
# JWT_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string

# --- Web dev server -----------------------------------------------------------
# Where Vite proxies /api to. Change it if you moved the API off port 4000.
# VITE_API_PROXY=http://localhost:4000

# --- Optional: compiled languages (Java, C, C++, Go) --------------------------
# JavaScript and Python need nothing. To also run Java, C, C++ or Go in the
# playground, point these at a Judge0 instance (self-hosted or RapidAPI's
# judge0-ce). Leave them unset and the app says so plainly rather than faking
# a result - JavaScript and Python keep working either way.
#
# These are read ONLY by the API server (server/index.js), never bundled into
# the browser - unlike a VITE_-prefixed variable, which Vite bakes into the
# shipped JS and anyone can read back out of it. Do not rename these to a
# VITE_JUDGE0_* form.
# JUDGE0_API_URL=https://judge0-ce.p.rapidapi.com
# JUDGE0_API_KEY=your_rapidapi_key
# JUDGE0_API_HOST=judge0-ce.p.rapidapi.com
#
# (A .env still using the old VITE_JUDGE0_API_URL / VITE_JUDGE0_API_KEY /
# VITE_JUDGE0_API_HOST names keeps working - the server falls back to those -
# but only the server ever reads them now; switch to the names above when
# it's convenient.)

# --- Optional: Gemini question assistant (admin console) ----------------------
# Lets the admin console's "AI new question" button use Google's Gemini to
# write a question from a plain-English description, say which stage it
# fits, and check whether the bank already covers it. Get a key from Google
# AI Studio (https://aistudio.google.com/apikey). The model defaults to
# gemini-2.5-flash; set GEMINI_MODEL to use another one.
#
# These are read ONLY by the API server (server/ai.js), never bundled into
# the browser - do not rename them to a VITE_* form. What is sent to Google:
# the admin's draft text, and existing question titles/prompts/snippets for
# duplicate checks and suggestions - never learner accounts, progress or
# any credential. Every AI draft still goes through the same validation and
# solution run as a hand-written question before it can be saved.
#
# Leave both unset and everything else keeps working; the button simply
# explains what is missing.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=your-google-ai-studio-key
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# --- Administrator account bootstrap -------------------------------------------
# The administrator is a completely separate account from every learner
# ("user") account: it has no email, is never a row in the users table, never
# appears in the leaderboard/learner statistics/Excel sync, and cannot be
# created by signing up and being "promoted" - it exists purely as its own
# Admin User ID + Admin Password, chosen by you below.
#
# Set BOTH the first time you run this project and start the server once:
#   1. it validates ADMIN_USER_ID (3-32 characters, letters/numbers/hyphens/
#      underscores only) and ADMIN_PASSWORD (12+ characters, rejecting a
#      handful of obviously weak passwords),
#   2. hashes ADMIN_PASSWORD with bcrypt,
#   3. creates the one administrator record, storing the Admin User ID and
#      the password HASH only - the plaintext password is never written to
#      disk anywhere,
#   4. and never touches these two variables again on any later restart, even
#      if you leave them set.
#
# Because of that last point, ADMIN_PASSWORD is safe to remove from .env
# immediately after the first successful startup - the server has already
# hashed it and does not need it again. Leaving it in .env is also fine
# (it will simply be ignored from then on); removing it is just slightly
# tidier and means the plaintext password isn't sitting in a local file
# longer than it needs to be. ADMIN_USER_ID can stay or go for the same
# reason - it's only ever read again if the administrator account is somehow
# deleted from the database entirely.
#
# To change the Admin User ID or Password later, sign in and use
# /admin/settings/security instead of editing these variables - that is the
# only supported way to change credentials once the account exists.
#
# Leave both unset (or empty, as they appear below) and the app still runs
# fine for learners; /admin/login simply has nothing to sign in with until
# you set these once and restart.
ADMIN_USER_ID=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Optional: Microsoft Excel sync (Microsoft Graph API) ---------------------
# Mirrors LEARNER signups/logins/progress into an Excel table for people who
# live in spreadsheets. The administrator account above is never included -
# this table is learner accounts only, and the sync code is never even given
# the administrator record to write. Devlingo's own database is ALWAYS the
# source of truth - Excel is a downstream, best-effort mirror; if any of this
# is unset, missing, or Microsoft is briefly unreachable, signup/login/
# progress all keep working exactly as before and the admin Excel Settings
# page says plainly that sync isn't configured or that a sync attempt failed.
# See server/excel.js.
#
# Setup: in Azure AD, register an application; grant it the APPLICATION
# (client-credentials) permission `Files.ReadWrite.All` under Microsoft Graph
# and have an admin grant consent; note the tenant ID, application (client) ID
# and a client secret you create for it. Then, in the OneDrive or SharePoint
# document library where your workbook lives, create a plain Excel table
# (select your header row, Insert > Table) with these columns, in this order:
#   User ID | Username | Email | Signup Date | Last Active | XP | Level |
#   Streak | Stages Completed | Challenges Completed | Is Premium
# This app fills that table in; it never creates the workbook, worksheet or
# table for you, and it is never given anything to write except the columns
# above - no password, password hash, JWT or other secret is ever synced, and
# no administrator credential of any kind either.
#
# These are read ONLY by the API server, never bundled into the browser - do
# not rename any of these to a VITE_* form.
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=your-azure-ad-tenant-id
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=your-app-registration-client-id
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-value
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_DRIVE_ID=the-onedrive-or-sharepoint-drive-id
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_WORKBOOK_ID=the-workbook-items-drive-item-id
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_WORKSHEET_NAME=Users
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_TABLE_NAME=Users
